            <ArticleTitle>Combined thoracic and hepatobiliary surgery for iatrogenic bronchobiliary fistula.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>218492317745747</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/0218492317745747</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Bronchobiliary fistula is a rare pathology mainly caused by hepatic tumors, bile duct obstruction, or hepatic hydatid disease. A 70-year-old man developed a bronchobiliary fistula after biliary stenting. After failure of conservative treatment including endoscopic retrograde biliary drainage, he underwent a combined operation with a two-level approach. Both a thoracotomy and laparotomy were performed, allowing pulmonary resection, diaphragmatic repair, and bile duct reconstruction during the same operation. Postoperative follow-up at one year showed optimal healing of the fistula.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
